  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n -  Noticeably cooler today, but breezy west winds are ushering \n    in thick smoke from regional wildfires. \n\n -  Cool morning temperatures Monday and Tuesday. \n\n -  A prolonged warming and drying trend this week.\n\n\nA dry cold front pushed through the Northern Rockies last night, \nleaving a much cooler air mass in its wake. High temperatures \ntoday will top out comfortably in the 70s to low 80s across most \nlocal valleys. However, breezy west to northwest winds will \npersist throughout the day. These continuous winds are pulling in \nthick smoke from highly active wildland fires across Washington, \nand smoke conditions will significantly worsen across the region \nby tonight.\n\nThis cooler and drier airmass will persist into early this week. \nWith clear skies and lighter winds expected overnight tonight and \nMonday night, very efficient radiational cooling is anticipated. \nHigh-elevation valleys and typically cold-prone locations are \nlikely to see temperatures drop into the mid-30s by early morning,\npresenting a chilly start for campers and early-morning \nrecreationists. Some high-resolution models continue to hint at a\nslight chance for scattered showers overnight into Monday morning\nacross northwest Montana, particularly near the Canadian border \nand Glacier National Park. \n\nA broad upper-level ridge of high pressure will rebuild across \nthe western United States this week. This pattern shift will \ninitiate a prolonged warming and drying trend across western \nMontana and north-central Idaho. This system is unlikely to tap \ninto North American Monsoon moisture at least through the end of \nthe week, meaning the atmosphere will remain dry. Expect valley \ntemperatures to climb back into the low to mid-90s by Wednesday \nand Thursday.",
